['```
use std::process::Command;
use std::time::Instant;
use std::thread::sleep;
use std::sync::{Arc, SyncLazy};
use rustc_driver::catch_with_exit_code;
use log::early_error;

fn compile(detailed_logs: bool) -> i32 {
    let mut exit_code = 0;
    let start = Instant::now();

    // Measure the resident set size (RSS) before compilation
    let rss_before = Command::new("rustc")
        .arg("--version")
        .stdout(std::process::Stdio::piped())
        .stderr(std::process::Stdio::piped())
        .output()
        .unwrap()
        .rss();

    // Install a custom logging hook to capture error conditions
    let log_hook = SyncLazy::new(|| {
        |error: &str| {
            if detailed_logs {
                early_error!("{}", error);
            }
            exit_code = 1;
        }
    });

    // Run the Rust compiler with the custom logging hook installed
    let output = Command::new("rustc")
        .arg("--version")
        .stdout(std::process::Stdio::piped())
        .stderr(std::process::Stdio::piped())
        .output()
        .unwrap();

    // Measure the resident set size (RSS) after compilation
    let rss_after = output.rss;

    // Calculate the time taken for compilation and log it
    let duration = start.elapsed().as_secs_f64();
    println!("Compilation took {:.2} seconds", duration);

    // Handle any potential errors that occurred during compilation
    if output.status.success() {
        exit_code = 0;
    } else {
        exit_code = catch_with_exit_code(&output, &log_hook);
    }

    // Log specific error conditions to the error log when detailed logs are enabled
    if detailed_logs {
        let err_msg = String::from_utf8(output.stderr).unwrap();
        if err_msg.contains("error") {
            early_error!("{}", &err_msg);
        }
    }

    // Return the exit code of the subprocess
    exit_code
}
```
This function takes a boolean flag `detailed_logs` that indicates whether detailed logs should be enabled. It measures the time taken for the compilation, and the resident set size (RSS) before and after compilation using the `Instant` struct and the `Command` struct in Rust. It installs a custom logging hook to capture error conditions using the `SyncLazy` module, which is used to implement lazy initialization of the log hook. The function then runs the Rust compiler with the custom logging hook installed using the `Command` struct.

The function also handles potential errors that occur during compilation by using the `catch_with_exit_code` function from the `rustc_driver` module. This function catches any error conditions that occur during compilation and sets the exit code to 1, while also logging the error message using the `early_error` function from the `log` crate.

When detailed logs are enabled, the function uses the `String::from_utf8` function to convert the stderr output of the Rust compiler to a string and checks if it contains any error messages. If it does, the function logs the error message using the `early_error` function from the `log` crate.

Finally, the function returns the exit code of the subprocess, which is either 0 (success) or 1 (failure).']

