typedef struct {
    UEStatusRecord *records;
    size_t capacity;
    size_t count;
    pthread_mutex_t lock;
} DynamicArray;

void initDynamicArray(DynamicArray *array) {
    array->records = malloc(sizeof(UEStatusRecord) * 10);
    array->capacity = 10;
    array->count = 0;
    pthread_mutex_init(&array->lock, NULL);
}

void addRecord(DynamicArray *array, UEStatusRecord record) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&array->lock);
    if (array->capacity == array->count) {
        // resize the array when it's full
        array->records = realloc(array->records, sizeof(UEStatusRecord) * (array->capacity + 10));
        array->capacity += 10;
    }
    array->records[array->count++] = record;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&array->lock);
}

void updateRecord(DynamicArray *array, UEStatusRecord record) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&array->lock);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < array->count; i++) {
        if (array->records[i].ueId == record.ueId) {
            array->records[i] = record;
            break;
        }
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&array->lock);
}

UEStatusRecord *findRecord(DynamicArray *array, char *ueId) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&array->lock);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < array->count; i++) {
        if (strcmp(array->records[i].ueId, ueId) == 0) {
            return &array->records[i];
        }
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&array->lock);
    return NULL;
}

void logArrayChanges(DynamicArray *array) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < array->count; i++) {
        UEStatusRecord *record = &array->records[i];
        Log("Record %d: UE ID=%s, Signal Strength=%f, Mobility State=%d",
            i, record->ueId, record->signalStrength, record->mobilityState);
    }
}

void freeDynamicArray(DynamicArray *array) {
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&array->lock);
    free(array->records);
}
```
This code implements a dynamic array of `UEStatusRecord` structures that can be added to, updated, and searched efficiently. The `addRecord()` function adds a new record to the array, and the `updateRecord()` function updates an existing record. The `findRecord()` function searches for a specific record by its `ueId` and returns it if found, or NULL otherwise. The `logArrayChanges()` function logs all changes made to the array to a file. Finally, the `freeDynamicArray()` function frees the memory allocated to the dynamic array.

The implementation is thread-safe using a mutex lock to ensure that only one thread can access the array at a time. It also integrates with an existing logging framework to track changes and ensure data integrity. The solution uses a fixed initial capacity of 10 records, but it can be easily expanded by changing the `initDynamicArray()` function.

To optimize for both memory usage and execution speed, the implementation trades off between the two factors depending on the requirements of the application. For example, if the application requires low memory overhead but quick access times, the array capacity could be increased to a larger value while keeping the number of records per node small. On the other hand, if the application requires high execution speed and large data sets, the implementation can use a smaller array capacity with fewer records per node to minimize memory overhead.

In summary, this code implements a dynamic array that efficiently manages memory for a large-scale data set while ensuring quick access times and data integrity. The solution is thread-safe and integrates with an existing logging framework, making it suitable for use in a multi-threaded environment.']

