In this example, we have defined a struct `NFAPI_LTE_PHY_PDU` that contains two fields: `tx_ue_data_pdu` and `mac_pdu`. We have also defined a function `validate_pdu` to validate the integrity of a PDU structure.

The functions `add_pdu` and `remove_pdu` are used to add or remove a PDU structure from the array, respectively. These functions perform similar operations to the `add_ue_instance` and `remove_ue_instance` functions for the UE instances.

The functions `add_ue_instance` and `remove_ue_instance` are used to add or remove an instance of a UE from the array, respectively. These functions perform similar operations to the `add_pdu` and `remove_pdu` functions for the PDU structures.

The main function in this example is the `main` function, which demonstrates how to use the above functions to create and manipulate an instance of a UE-PDU array. The program first creates an instance of the UE-PDU array using the `create_ue_pdu_array` function, then adds a new PDU structure to the array using the `add_pdu` function. It then removes the last PDU structure from the array using the `remove_pdu` function and frees the memory allocated for it. Finally, it removes the last UE instance from the array using the `remove_ue_instance` function and frees the memory allocated for it.

Note that in this example, we have defined the functions `add_pdu` and `remove_pdu` to handle PDU structures, while the functions `add_ue_instance` and `remove_ue_instance` are used to handle UE instances. This is because the two types of structures have different memory management requirements. In a real-world scenario, you may need to define separate functions for each type of structure to handle their memory management more efficiently.']

